ture  so those are our three panelists I think  we're in for a lively debate here so  without further ado I'm going to turn to  you Barry and that I think the  overarching question we're asking here  at the beginning is could biological sex  itself be an illusion  constructed from human categories I'm  looking over at Barry's notes the title  of his notes a sex pitch mainly about  genetics when we get to the debate  because it's long being my own in focus  of interest when looking at the sciences  biological sex in particular has  interested me now way back in 1967 the  sex of a newborn was fixed by visual  inspection and a register of births  certified its status or meal made as  male or female tall they were allowed to  do but sighted geneticists at this time  had begun to use a quick and simple test  to determine the genetic sex they did  thousands of applications of this simple  thing mostly were normal cases but they  they identified quite a few anomalous  ones by use of the genetic tests and  that made the genetics of sex much more  complicated than it had been and  researchers realized that conveying what  they knew have sex two others have  become at the same time much more  difficult and much more important now of  course it couldn't have been predicted  then that in just a few decades we would  live in a world where there in sex is  mostly referred to as gender and  genetics is mostly molecular genetics  I've watched accounts of genetic sex  become ever more detailed and complex  over a long period and I've seen nothing  to suggest that sex as biological sex is  an illusion although it has been said  and is being said in another debate that  the world's all an illusion and how  would we know if it were as two entries  on birth certificates however they are  neither empirical  descriptions like a empirical science  would make nor are they illusions which  seems to be the only alternative that  comes to the mind of many people their  statuses their attributions of status  that indicate to other members of  society how they ought to treat the  person designated and assigned and if  other statuses join will replace those  in current use  nothing changes they're still all  statuses they don't describe empirical  States even if they're based on  empirical States now it may be that the  registrar's of tomorrow in a more  tolerant society are going to do an  awful lot better assigning sex statuses  than those who long ago assigned a sex  to an intersex newborn and kept that  assignation confidential but even they  did no more long ago than attach the  status to a child and act with her mind  to how she was treated in the future we  need to understand statuses thank you  very much so it's a story of increasing  complexity in our understanding in  general but this concept of the status  is what's key to what you're arguing  thank you very much Daphna let me come  to you  so there's a short answer to the  question as the short answer is I don't  think that biological sex is an illusion  but I do think that our binary  conceptualization of sex is an illusion  and the long answer is that this is a  tricky question  because we use biological sex for at  least two different meanings we use  biological sex sex to describe the  divisions of humans into a sex  categories male and female according to  the form of the genitalia by imposing a  binary division on on the form of the  genitalia we ignore or we mistreat  individuals that do not fit into one of  these categories so whereas most humans  or most of humans genitalia do fit into  the categories because they have only  made typical organs or only female  typical organs some humans don't now the  other meaning that we use biological sex  is to relate to all the genes and  hormones that are involved in the  formation of the genitalia and they of  course exist these genes and hormones  but the binary frame will framework  misleads us to believe that these genes  and hormones are arranged in two  distinct sets what one appearing in  males and the other appearing with  females and this is simply not true so  except from a few genes on the Y  chromosomes all humans share all the  genes and the hormones that are involved  in the formation of the genitalia and  they these genes and hormones especially  the hormones are highly dynamic highly  reactive highly overlapping between  humans and they clearly do not come in  two distinct form like the genitalia do  now these genes and hormones affect not  just genitalia but also other systems  and one of them is the brain and again  the the a binary framework miss leads us  to believe that sex effects on the day  on the brain add up consistently within  individuals as sex effects on the  genitalia often does to create distinct  male brain and female brains and this  again is not true because sex effects on  the brain usually mix up so each one of  us has feature  in the brains that are more common in  males in others that are more common in  females so zone sex  affects the brain and although there are  group level differences between the  brains of men and women they do not  adapt to create male brains and female  brains but rather mosaic brains thank  you very much so going back to your  early phrase there something maybe the  sex isn't an illusion but the binary  construct is perhaps an illusion okay  thank you  that's true it let me just push back  slightly on the binary thing and  obviously this is a topic which everyone  gets very upset about and it totally is  understandable because as both Barry and  Athena said we talked about sex in terms  of this thing which we study in the lab  and also in terms of this thing which is  super important in society and it's how  we label people and it's it's often how  we treat people as well so it's  completely understandable there's a lot  of bad feeling around this topic people  on both sides you know people who argue  against the importance of sex  differences 